                "username": "williamspatrick"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "community-membership.md",
                    "line": 207,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "This seems to offer some wiggle room. Who determine whether it really happens? A TOF vote? Or should we simply say \"will be removed\". I think there are some repos which are important but not actively developed in which it makes sense to allow a maintainer to remain, assuming they are responsive to any changes, even if they are not actively committing to OpenBMC anymore. If there is an option to not remove someone, I think we should define the process in which we do remove them so it doesn't appear arbitrary."
                },
                {
                    "file": "community-membership.md",
                    "line": 207,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I can change to will or shall if you like."
                },
                {
                    "file": "community-membership.md",
                    "line": 207,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> This seems to offer some wiggle room. Who determine whether it really happens? A TOF vote? Or should we simply say \"will be removed\".\n\nThe overall intent of this document is to document \"what\" not \"how\".  I'd like to keep it that way if we can just so that the \"how\" can evolve over time without needing additional process that only one or two people really need to execute on.\n\nI suspect \"can\" wiggle room is intentional by patrick, but I'd be in support of either way.  I think the _MUST_ in the previous sentence is the more important one.\n\n> I think there are some repos which are important but not actively developed in which it makes sense to allow a maintainer to remain, assuming they are responsive to any changes, even if they are not actively committing to OpenBMC anymore. If there is an option to not remove someone, I think we should define the process in which we do remove them so it doesn't appear arbitrary.\n\nIs there a concrete example of this?  I would assume that maintainers that are responding to changes would meet the bar for active in a 6 month period, but I agree, there's a theoretical grey area.  I don't think it's worth trying to change this commit to try to address that."
                },
                {
                    "file": "community-membership.md",
                    "line": 207,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> I think there are some repos which are important but not actively developed in which it makes sense to allow a maintainer to remain, assuming they are responsive to any changes, even if they are not actively committing to OpenBMC anymore. \n\nI would suggest that we should still mark them not as an active maintainer.  If someone is only focused on that one repository that was developed years ago, and not actively engaging with new changes or elsewhere in the project, how do they satisfy this?\n\n```\n* Work with other subproject owners to maintain the project's overall health and success holistically.\n* Keep abreast of technical changes to the overall project and maintain and/or delegate maintenance of the subproject to keep it aligned with the overall project.\n```"
                },
                {
                    "file": "community-membership.md",
                    "line": 207,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> mark them not as an active maintainer. \n\nAgreed."
                },
                {
                    "file": "community-membership.md",
                    "line": 207,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                }
            ],
